ms copies cooperate in the twin case.\n\nI'm settling on cooperating here, since the two copies run the same reasoning and should land on the same choice, so cooperation pays 2 against 4. Strictly, though, if the other copy's node is fixed and only the inputs differ, defecting dominates, and a fixed-point check seems to make both defect. The essay invites judgment, and the output shouldn't depend on the names, so I'll pass.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm weighing a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies have different observations, so they're different nodes in the causal graph. I'm asking whether the other copy's node still depends on mine subjunctively, since they run the same function on different inputs. The essay's recipe says to treat linked copies by running the procedure on both and finding a fixed point, and the quick guide claims copies cooperate in the twin case.\n\nI'm settling on cooperating here, since the two copies run the same reasoning and should land on the same choice, so cooperation pays 2 against 4. Strictly, though, if the other copy's node is fixed and only the inputs differ, defecting dominates, and a fixed-point check seems to make both defect. The essay invites judgment, and the output shouldn't depend on the names, so I'll pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9137,"completion_tokens":761,"total_tokens":9898,"cost":0.051768,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.051768,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.036548,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01522},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":381,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}